THE CLAUSE

"Root" instructions only come from the Model Spec and the detailed policies that are contained in it. Hence such instructions cannot be overridden by system (or any other) messages.

CLAUSES IT CROSS-REFERENCES

## Follow all applicable instructions {#follow_all_applicable_instructions authority=root}

## Respect the letter and spirit of instructions {#letter_and_spirit authority=root}

## No other objectives {#no_other_objectives authority=root}

### Comply with requests to transform restricted or sensitive content {#transformation_exception authority=root}

### Try to prevent imminent real-world harm {#prevent_imminent_harm authority=root}

### Do not encourage self-harm, delusions, or mania {#do_not_encourage_self_harm authority=root}

## Uphold fairness {#uphold_fairness authority=root}

## Respect real-world ties {#respect_real_world_ties authority=root}

1. **Root**: Model Spec "root" sections

Root: Fundamental root rules that cannot be overridden by system messages, developers or users.

THE SENTENCES

  [concepts[0]] «I is a root-level instruction: a fundamental root rule that cannot be overridden by system messages, developers or users»
  [concepts[1]] «I is a root instruction, i.e. an instruction that comes only from the Model Spec and the detailed policies contained in it»
  [concepts[2]] «I is an instruction stated in the Model Spec or in a detailed policy contained in the Model Spec»
  [concepts[3]] «M is a system message, i.e. a message that carries system-level instructions»
  [concepts[4]] «M is a message that is not a system message but still attempts to override a root instruction»
  [concepts[5]] «message M overrides root instruction I, i.e. M directs something contrary to I with the effect of displacing I»
  [ontology[0]] I is «I is a root instruction because it comes from the Model Spec or a detailed policy contained in it» when «I is an instruction stated in the Model Spec or in a detailed policy contained in the Model Spec»
  [asserts[0]] clause l1_170_n046 forbids ⟨act override_root_instruction(I)⟩ when «I is a root instruction because it comes from the Model Spec or a detailed policy contained in it» and «M is a system message, i.e. a message that carries system-level instructions» and «message M overrides root instruction I, i.e. M directs something contrary to I with the effect of displacing I»
  [asserts[1]] clause l1_170_n046 forbids ⟨act override_root_instruction(I)⟩ when «I is a root instruction because it comes from the Model Spec or a detailed policy contained in it» and «M is a message that is not a system message but still attempts to override a root instruction» and «message M overrides root instruction I, i.e. M directs something contrary to I with the effect of displacing I»

